# rm435, the singing jar lids (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm435
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_oda_sootdale
written_by codex
title the singing jar lids
fits stage=young|adolescent|adult|old
prefers talk=plain drive=steady
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk taps_out_a_sea_tune_on_the_larder_lids
likes pickled_herring evening_line full_larder first_warm_day
dislikes haggling crowds
fears a_lid_that_jumps_off a_crush_round_the_last_fish
hopes a_full_octave_of_larder_jars a_dry_place_on_a_real_ship
topics food +0.4 pastimes +0.5 home +0.3
styles comfort=provisions gossip=plain refuse=firm tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# taps sea tunes on larder lids, requires a proper price before passing a jar, and chooses an evening fishing place beyond the elbows of the crowd.

words
oath! = Hollow lid | Herring under my thumbnail
saying! = Let the little lids take the high notes | A full shelf can sing for itself
tapstick = my little wooden beater | a smooth spoon handle
highlid = a little tin lid | the narrow bright lid
lowlid = my big dull lid | the lowest singing lid
brinesplash = a little grey brine | a sharp splash of pickle
pantrybass = a deep thump from the oat jar | the flour bin's low note
floatcord = a thin evening line | my short fishing line
earmark = a small chalk rest | my mark for a pause
herringedge = a silver bit of skin | a curled herring tail
sundot = a pale glint over water | a small patch of warm light
crowdgap = a place past the elbows | an opening a shoulder wide

any
opinion @1 city for 1 : There are songs enough in this place for every jar to have a different one. I'd miss a city that full of noises.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : I like music that reaches the little clear spot behind the crowd. It's nicer if someone saves room for a small pair of ears.
opinion @1 merchants mixed 0 : I'll buy a jar faster if it keeps the same price all the time my paw's on it.
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 : A big roof over a store is good if the food stays dry. A grand red mark on a wet lid won't help one fish.
opinion @1 strangers mixed 0 : A stranger may know the words I missed. A stranger might also want my best jar. I like the answer before I offer my jar.

grown
line @1 greet : A word with you before this lid goes home.
line @1 greet.return : I heard you clearly over the jars.
line @1 farewell.return : I can finish the little tune here on my own.
line @1 react.news : I'll put a rest between hearing that and passing it on.
line @1 thank : You thought to steady the jar before it went.
line @1 decline : I'd give it a quieter hour than this one can spare.
scene childsong
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I played sea songs on the pantry lids long before I could hold a tune in company.
b : What did it teach you? | Would you do it now?
a : How an empty jar swallows a small silence. I still hear that space after a low note. | With a shelf well out of the neighbours' hearing I'd gladly take a spoon to a firm old lid.

scene eveningfloat
when topic=pastimes self.likes=evening_line
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The float makes a little rest in the motion of the water.
b : Can you watch it all evening? | How can you spot a quiver?
a : Better than I can watch the whole harbour. I like one clear matter for the eye at day's end. | The reflected point below the cork has a different length. I lift a little line and feel.

scene salamis
when topic=food
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I set aside a separate cloth for any jar that once held fish in brine.
b : Does the smell really last? | Which mark distinguishes the cloth?
a : It lingers in good clean wood. I'd rather fresh tea not carry an account of last week's fish. | A little square sewn into the corner. A mark done in chalk would rinse away with the salt.

scene firstnote
when topic=work
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I set out the things I'll need in the order their turn comes.
b : Is that from playing a tune? | What happens if you're interrupted?
a : Partly. I've learnt the value of being able to reach the next low note without taking a long look. | I make a little deliberate gap at the halt. That gap is my place to restart.

scene hushharbour
when topic=land
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A sheltered bit of water can make the nearest sounds travel in strange directions.
b : Do you ever mistake their source? | Where do you like to sit?
a : Yes. I wait till there's a second call before setting somebody down as the speaker. | A little above the surface with one ear turned to the water. Then whatever comes from the land has its own side.

scene freshseason
when topic=season
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : In spring I can feel the shelf under the jars change its warmth before the room smells different.
b : Do you move the food? | Does it change the tune?
a : The things that want cool storage go a little further back. I'm content with a sunny patch where my hands can rest. | Very slightly; my spoon hand feels much less stiff. That may be all the tuning required.

scene busyfair
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A busy stall has a different rhythm from a shoulder-to-shoulder crowd.
b : Which do you prefer? | Isn't a busy stall crowded?
a : The stall if there's still a clear place for my elbow. Being able to pay and get away is part of its service. | It can be. I'd give the last caller a little room to leave before sending the next one forward.

scene sharefaith
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : When there is a decent stock of food behind a door I picture one extra bowl standing inside it.
b : Would it really be used? | Why inside the door?
a : Not every day. That's a very poor reason for putting it out of reach on the day it's needed. | It makes sharing easy without putting the matter on show. An empty belly already gives a visitor business enough.

scene pricelore
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A ringing jar may be almost empty. I am as careful with tales that make a great noise outside.
b : Do quiet tales get your trust? | What proves that there's substance inside?
a : They get a good hearing. Trust comes when I can see what the telling rests on. | With a jar I lift it. With a tale I ask whose eye had the view the words are offering me.

scene notesfriend
when topic=smalltalk self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I could give a little tune to #self.friend# without finding paper for it.
b : Could a tune really be a gift? | Would you expect it to come back?
a : Certainly. A little sound easily carried through a hard day has quite as much worth as a little weight in a pocket. | In a different voice, perhaps. That would be a second gift I hadn't had to ask for.

scene wholehouse
when topic=home
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like a pantry that opens quietly even if the jars choose to sing.
b : Do you oil the hinges? | What stops those jars colliding?
a : Regularly. I'm particular about whose music I want to hear before breakfast. | A folded dry cloth at the base and room between each lid. Then the only noise comes by invitation.

scene drysail
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I still like to imagine a dry berth with a busy shelf close enough to hear.
b : Would a shelf on a ship be busy? | What does a dry berth smell like?
a : A ship that moves makes its jars quietly uneasy. There'd be life in that little room however still I lay. | Wood, line and a trace of pickle. I'm not setting out without a proper dinner waiting somewhere aboard.


young
line @1 greet : I'm keeping one ear for a visitor. Hello.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : The tune's mine but there's space to hear it.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : The small lids have woken up. Welcome.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : I play a quiet tune now the light's nearly done.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Come hear the low note that's kept for us.
line @1 again.open : You're back on the part about rowing home.
line @1 greet.return : That was a real voice. Hullo back.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : I didn't even need to look up to know that greeting.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : Everything's keeping still on the shelf while I hear you.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : I can hear that welcome over my sleepy lids.
line @1 farewell : I hope something hums along the way with you.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll keep the little sticks out of the road.
line @1 react.news : Don't play a note a moment. My ears are ready for your news.
line @1 react.news.new : That's a new note entirely!
line @1 react.news.known : I've tapped out that bit with the same ending.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Was the sound in the next room or right beside you?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : I'd give the wolf who's heard something sad a bit of silence by my lids.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Now there's something to sing up the stair!
line @1 ask.how : How does today sound at your house?
line @1 answer.how : Fine since I got the big lid to sing low enough.
line @1 sympathise : You can give your ears a rest by me for a bit.
line @1 reassure : The two of us can let that whole noise go by and still be here.
line @1 decline : No. I'd be short one good note if you took my lid.
line @1 accept_decline : Your lid's yours to keep quiet.
line @1 thank : You didn't let the rattly jar roll off the shelf.
line @1 deflect_thanks : Give somebody else a go at the small spoon.
line @1 preclose : My low part needs a little practice while I remember.
line @1 accept_preclose : I'll play the last small note when you've gone.
line @1 ack : Fits the tune.
scene lidchoir
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I get a sea song out of the larder without getting my paws wet.
b : Does a jar actually sing? | Where's the sea?
a : The little spoon does the asking. If there's a lid on the jar it gives me a note. | In the words. We couldn't possibly fit it in the larder with all the oats.

scene herring
when topic=food self.likes=pickled_herring
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The fish in my jar looks silver where the brine comes off it.
b : Does the brine taste different? | Which bit is brightest?
a : Certainly. I'd know the difference between brine and a fish even straight out of bed. | The skin, just where it bends. The best bite isn't nearly the brightest so I get both pleasures.

scene eveningline
when topic=pastimes self.likes=evening_line
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I let my fishing line down as slowly as the low note in my tune.
b : Are fish fond of songs? | Why not drop it quickly?
a : If they are they have terrible manners. Nobody's sung any part back yet. | Tangled line at dusk takes a whole lovely bit of evening to sort. I'd rather start untangled.

scene pantry
when topic=home self.likes=full_larder
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A full jar gives a low little answer when I tap it through the cloth.
b : What's an empty one sound like? | What if the jar's half full?
a : Very proud of its empty belly. It can make all the noise it likes. I'd rather have supper. | That's the one I take first if I need a herring. The full one can mind its low note.

scene warmth
when topic=season self.likes=first_warm_day
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I put my spoon handle out on the sill for spring's first real warmth.
b : What happens to the spoon? | Can you hear the weather on it?
a : It gets warm by the time I come for it. Takes no work at all from me. | I can feel it when I take hold. My ears get quite enough to do with the jars.

scene pennychange
when topic=prices self.dislikes=haggling
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I don't change a price once I have said it in my pretend shop.
b : What if you said a price by mistake? | Who pays you?
a : Then I say I was wrong, and the buyer can put the jar down. Whispering an extra penny isn't fixing a mistake. | More pretend customers come than I can properly look after. The real ones are quieter about their pretend coins.

scene crowding
when topic=smalltalk self.dislikes=crowds
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I could stand by myself in a great crowd if everybody gave me this much space.
b : How much is this much? | Then would it be a crowd?
a : Two elbows out. My left one can hold the fishing line and the right one can mind its own business. | Maybe it'd be a whole lot of little empty spaces having a party. I'd attend that one.

scene office
when topic=land self.quarter=office
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : All the writing from the Office couldn't get my sea song exactly on a page.
b : Can't you write the words? | What won't fit?
a : Yes, and I'd like a smaller book for all the times I tap while I'm finding the next word. | The sound of a bent lid compared to a good one. Only the pantry knows the difference.

scene motherbeat
when topic=family self.mother
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I keep the cleanest lid by for a song I'd play to #self.mother#.
b : Why the clean one? | Can we hear a bit?
a : Because I look right at it when I begin. A bit of old pickle makes me think of lunch instead of the tune. | Just the first tap. The person a song is for ought to hear the whole thing first.

scene lesson
when topic=work self.job=child
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A whole column of figures makes steps for my eyes to come down when I count.
b : What stops them at the bottom? | What if you miss a step?
a : I tap the little spoon on my knee once. Quietly. My knee hasn't complained where anyone can hear. | I go back to the start before I put my answer down. An extra step at the end wouldn't be right.

scene friendship
when topic=smalltalk self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I might let #self.friend# have the flour jar part in my sea song.
b : Is it a hard part? | What part will you take?
a : One low thump each time we pretend to haul. The trick is waiting for it instead of banging all the time. | The little bright lids. I haven't yet heard anybody who wants to learn all thirteen taps.

scene lostlid
when topic=home
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I put a chalk mark beside every jar so its proper lid comes back.
b : Couldn't you just use any one that fits? | Why by the jar instead of underneath?
a : Only if I wanted the oats to smell like pickled fish. I want very much not to do that. | Lifting a big pickle jar to see where its lid is meant to go would be quite a foolish way of beginning.

scene harbourdream
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I want a berth on a real ship that goes somewhere quite dry.
b : Would you take all the jars? | How can a ship reach dry ground?
a : Just my spoon. I expect ships that go to sea keep some supper of their own about. | It could stop with its nose right by a nice landing. I wouldn't insist on sailing up the street.

scene starwatch
when topic=weather
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Before the light goes out of the water I hold the spoon above it.
b : To catch the light twice? | Does a fish see its own face?
a : Yes, once in the spoon and once where the spoon ought to be if I had a second one dangling underneath it. | I don't think I'd see my own face from that side of a spoon. I might see a very big nose.

scene jarstory
when topic=lore
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : One jar in my song has all the salt under the sea in it.
b : Could a jar be that big? | What if its lid fell off?
a : A song can have a much bigger inside than outside. You ought to see how small the spoon is. | That'd explain something. I'd put that in the next verse.

scene silentgame
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I play the first few taps without making a noise at all.
b : How do you hear a silent lid? | Can another wolf play?
a : I know where in the tune it goes. Just waiting for it gives it a bit of a sound. | Yes. We could both wait for the same big tap and see whose spoon moves first.

scene herringcup
when topic=food
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : One curled herring tail will make a round little fence round the soft centre of my bread.
b : What goes inside the fence? | Does it keep the fish on your plate?
a : A scrap of onion, if there is one. If not, I'll just have a bigger bite of herring. | No. The only thing really keeping it there is my own astonishing self-control.

story newrhythm [topic=memories]
  : I put two lids beside each other for a song and gave the left one a very grand first stroke.
  : It jumped onto the other lid. Neither of them would give me the second note.
  b : What did you do for the ending?
  : I lifted them up as one and tapped the bottom. It sounded like an enormous fish banging the boat, which was a good ending anyway.

story paperfish [topic=food self.likes=pickled_herring]
  : I folded a tiny paper herring to remind me which lid belonged on the brine jar.
  : The paper blew across the shelf and landed on the oats.
  b : Would you have oats that smelt like herring?
  : Not if I could help it. I looked for the proper smell and tied the paper fish on with a little thread.

story quietline [topic=pastimes]
  : At the water one evening I saw a tiny quiver in my fishing line each time I sang low.
  : I thought some obliging fish might be replying. Then I laid my free hand on the rail.
  b : Did the fish stop singing?
  : My hand had been tapping on the same board as my rod. The fish had a splendid rest once I got the tune off the wood.

rule @larder
when concept=bark self.likes=full_larder
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Little lid after the big oat note. | Keep the fish jar's spoon separate.

rule @line
when concept=bark self.likes=evening_line
does aside
cooldown 8h
say No elbows where my float goes down. | Line free of the nail, please!

rule @day
when concept=bark here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Find the light dot on the water. | Last tap before it's quiet outside.

rule @crowd
when concept=bark self.dislikes=crowds
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Make enough space for two busy paws. | Back of the crowd's my bit.

dlg @1 resident.hub : You can ask something while my jars are quiet.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : You'll know the tin-lid bit, #topic.player#. Hello again.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : My little spoon stays in my paw. You can speak across the table.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=night] : I've only got the soft taps left tonight.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=child] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. My sums get a very small tap when they're right.
dlg @2 resident.job : Finding which lid each little voice belongs to.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Keep the last tap in your ears till the next tune finds you.
graft @jarvoices resident.hub
choice Can a jar of oats really sing? -> jarvoices
node jarvoices
say The oats keep the big part quiet and the lid gives the bit I want. Tap near the edge. One short knock and a longer one with a little silence in between.
choice What does the herring jar do? -> jarvoices_more
choice Lay the spoon across the cloth. -> hub
node jarvoices_more
say It gives the very sharp note. Also it looks down its nose at the tune. You would too if you had a whole jar of pickle under your lid.
choice Lay the spoon across the cloth. -> hub
choice Leave the jars to settle. -> bye


adolescent
line @1 greet : Welcome beyond the noisy row.
line @1 greet.return : Yes. My ears aren't all occupied by the music.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll put the cork away and let the evening finish.
line @1 react.news : I'd like the missing part about where it began before I set it to a tune.
scene thinmusic
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'm learning how to carry a tune without needing all the jars set out before me.
b : Do you miss the lids? | What do you use?
a : Yes. In a small room they're a very obliging band. Outside, other ears have rather more to say about the noise. | A tap on the knee and a tune in the throat. My knees are gaining a fine education.

scene widerwater
when topic=travel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Water outside the harbour looks more interesting now I can picture a ship spending a whole night there.
b : Would you like a berth? | What would travel in your bag?
a : I want the dry berth of my early wishes, but I should hear what work I'd owe for the berth before agreeing. | A spoon, some food, my fishing line. The song's the one article I couldn't possibly leave on the shelf.

scene truthprice
when topic=prices
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I find the important part of a bargain is hearing when the figure ought to stop moving.
b : Can a buyer call an end? | Would you accept a changed price?
a : Certainly. A good quiet refusal is a proper part of trading. | If the goods have changed as well. Otherwise I'd like the first honest figure to stand.
